<h1>The A1200: Release Date, Specs and What's Included</h1>
<p><em>Last updated: 26 November 2026</em></p>
<div class="detail-content">

<p>Retro Games Ltd. has been drip-feeding details about The A1200, a full-size reimagining of the classic Amiga 1200, for well over a year. By now most of the open questions have been answered officially, so this is a summary of everything that's confirmed, as of November 2026.</p>

<h2>Release date and price</h2>
<p>The A1200 was originally announced with a June 2026 delivery, but was pushed back several times. It now launches on <strong>Tuesday 15 December 2026</strong>, simultaneously in all regions, at a suggested price of <strong>189.99 Euro</strong>. The latest slip was caused by longer logistics routes via the warehouses, including Rotterdam. To make up for it, Retro Games Ltd. is offering <strong>Damocles: Mercenary II</strong> as a free download at launch. A USB-C power cable is included, but the AC adapter is not, so you'll need your own power supply or a phone charger.</p>

<h2>Hands-on impressions</h2>
<p>Pre-production units have been shown at Gamescom and covered by Retro Gamer, and the first hands-on reports are positive. They praise the build quality of the case and especially the typing feel of the fully functional keyboard. One detail worth knowing: the floppy slot on the right side is cosmetic only and closed off inside, so it is not a working drive.</p>

<h2>Workbench and software</h2>
<p>The biggest addition since the initial announcement is that the machine ships with <strong>Workbench 3.1</strong> integrated, unlike the A500 Mini. At power-on you can choose whether to boot straight into the game carousel or into the classic Workbench interface. Other AmigaOS versions can also be booted from a USB stick, so a preferred release can be selected.</p>

<h2>Emulation coverage</h2>
<p>Rather than a single fixed core, The A1200 emulates several Amiga configurations:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Amiga 1200 (AGA)</strong>, the headline machine.</li>
<li><strong>Amiga 500 (OCS)</strong>, <strong>Amiga 600 (ECS)</strong> and the <strong>CD32</strong>.</li>
<li><strong>RTG support</strong> (ReTargetable Graphics), which lets compatible applications run at higher resolutions than the original chipset allowed.</li>
<li>RAM, CPU and chipset settings are adjustable, so different Amiga hardware configurations can be reproduced.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Loading your own software</h2>
<p>Legally acquired Amiga software can be sideloaded via USB stick. This supports full <strong>WHDLoad</strong> packages as well as classic disk, hard-drive and CD-ROM images (<strong>ADF</strong>, <strong>HDF</strong> and CD images). Games can be saved and resumed at any time, which helps with the punishingly difficult classics.</p>

<h2>Games</h2>
<p>There are 25 games pre-installed, launched from a simple carousel. The final line-up spans classics and newer ports:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Highlights and new releases:</strong> The Settlers II (Gold Edition, the modern Amiga port from 2025), Reshoot Proxima 3 (2023), and the complete Turrican trilogy (Turrican, Turrican II, Turrican III).</li>
<li><strong>Adventures and role-playing:</strong> Beneath a Steel Sky, Lure of the Temptress, Mercenary: Escape from Targ.</li>
<li><strong>Action and skill:</strong> Alien Breed: Tower Assault, Ruff 'n' Tumble, Slam Tilt (AGA pinball).</li>
<li><strong>Cinemaware classics:</strong> Defender of the Crown, Defender of the Crown II, It Came from the Desert, Antheads: It Came from the Desert II, Wings, Rocket Ranger, Lords of the Rising Sun, Sinbad and the Throne of the Falcon, The King of Chicago, S.D.I., plus the four TV Sports titles (Baseball, Basketball, Boxing, Football).</li>
</ul>
<p>Each game gets four save slots, while The Settlers II adds six in-game save slots. Controls can be remapped, assigning controller buttons and analogue inputs to keyboard, mouse and joystick functions.</p>

<h2>Image and display options</h2>
<p>The HDMI output runs at 720p and supports both 50 Hz (PAL) and 60 Hz (NTSC), along with different scaling modes, CRT shaders and an intelligent smart crop.</p>

<h2>Hardware and ports</h2>
<p>The A1200 keeps the original full-size design with a fully working keyboard. There are <strong>four USB-A ports</strong> for gamepads, joysticks, mice and USB flash drives, and third-party controllers and keyboards are supported. With an appropriate adapter, even original Amiga peripherals can be used.</p>

<h2>What's in the box</h2>
<ul>
<li>The A1200 home computer</li>
<li>THEMOUSE, a 2-button USB mouse</li>
<li>THEGAMEPAD, an 8-button USB gamepad</li>
<li>USB-C power supply cable (AC adapter not included)</li>
<li>HDMI cable</li>
<li>Quickstart manual</li>
</ul>

<h2>Context: where this fits in the retro landscape</h2>
<p>The A1200 sits in a growing market of mini-consoles and FPGA recreations. Unlike MiSTer FPGA, which aims for cycle-accurate hardware reproduction, the A1200 is a software emulation box, similar to the A500 Mini that Retro Games Ltd. released in 2022. The A500 Mini was well-received but had its quirks, and the A1200 addresses some of those criticisms, particularly with the working keyboard and expanded I/O.</p>
<p>There's a legitimate debate about software emulation versus FPGA in 2026, and some of the Amiga community is waiting for a full FPGA-based Amiga. But at 189.99 Euro, the A1200 is an approachable plug-and-play entry point that ships with a real Workbench and strong sideloading support.</p>

<h2>What this means for the Amiga community</h2>
<p>The original hardware is getting harder to find in good condition, and the capacitor-bomb motherboard issue means that even working units need recapping. A modern, reliable reproduction, even as software emulation, lowers the barrier significantly. The 25 pre-installed games give newcomers a curated starting point, and the USB sideloading support means the library can be expanded without hardware modifications.</p>

